id=adpack
label=ADPACK / OpenMX (Ozaki)
families=nc
provenance=library
generator=adpack
engine=adpack
upstream=http://www.openmx-square.org/adpack/adpack.html
author=Taisuke Ozaki
reference=http://www.openmx-square.org/adpack/adpack.html
license=GPL-2.0-or-later
summary=Norm-conserving pseudopotentials for OpenMX, generated by ADPACK from its own per-element parameter decks.
notes=PseudoForge bundles the 29 input decks the ADPACK 2.2 distribution ships under work/ -- the author's radii, reference configurations and pseudization schemes, unmodified -- and runs ADPACK on them. The published decks set calc.type = vps, so ADPACK writes the OpenMX .vps potential only -- not the pseudo-atomic orbital basis (that would need calc.type = PAO or ALL). Output is OpenMX .vps, not UPF; Quantum ESPRESSO cannot read it. There is deliberately no synthesised fallback for elements outside those 29: an ADPACK deck fixes the scheme, the local channel and per-channel radii that were chosen by hand, and inventing them would mean issuing a PseudoForge construction under ADPACK's name. The released ADPACK 2.2 segfaults partway through every element on a default 8 MB stack; PseudoForge builds from a patched tree that moves about 9 MB of radial work arrays in MBK() to the heap. That patch changes no physics -- see docs/ENGINES.md and the fork's VERIFIED.md. One of the 29, tellurium, is declined by ADPACK's own Troullier-Martins root-finder on the radii as shipped; the deck is left as distributed rather than retuned.
available=29 elements: Ag Ar Au Be Br Ca Cl Cr F Ge He Hg K Kr Mg Na Nb Ne P Pb Pt S Sc Se Sn Te Ti V Zr. LDA and GGA.
# xc_available: ADPACK has one LDA (Ceperley-Alder, Perdew-Zunger fit) and one GGA (PBE). pw and pbesol were declared and produced those two under other names.
xc_available=pz pbe
# rel_available: All 29 shipped decks set eq.type dirac. The scalar and nonrel runs produced the same fully relativistic files under other names.
rel_available=full
families_note=NC, 29 elements, OpenMX format
formats=openmx
